.TH "ELEMENT.SCROLLINTOVIEW" "3JS" "July 9, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.scrollIntoView \- Element: scrollIntoView() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]scrollIntoView()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Element\f[R] interface scrolls the element\(cqs ancestor
containers such that the element on which \f[CR]scrollIntoView()\f[R] is
called is visible to the user.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
scrollIntoView()
scrollIntoView(alignToTop)
scrollIntoView(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]alignToTop\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A boolean value:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[CR]true\f[R], the top of the element will be aligned to the top of
the visible area of the scrollable ancestor.
Corresponds to
\f[CR]scrollIntoViewOptions: {block: \(dqstart\(dq, inline: \(dqnearest\(dq}\f[R].
This is the default value.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[CR]false\f[R], the bottom of the element will be aligned to the
bottom of the visible area of the scrollable ancestor.
Corresponds to
\f[CR]scrollIntoViewOptions: {block: \(dqend\(dq, inline: \(dqnearest\(dq}\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object with the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]behavior\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Determines whether scrolling is instant or animates smoothly.
This option is a string that must take one of the following values:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]smooth\f[R]: The scrolling animates smoothly.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]instant\f[R]: The scrolling happens instantly in a single jump.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]auto\f[R]: The scroll behavior is determined by the computed value
of the \f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] CSS property on the element.
If omitted, \f[CR]behavior\f[R] defaults to \f[CR]auto\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]block\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Defines the vertical alignment of the element within the scrollable
ancestor container.
Its value can be one of the following:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]start\f[R]: Aligns the element\(cqs top edge with the top of the
scrollable container, making the element appear at the start of the
visible area vertically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]center\f[R]: Aligns the element vertically at the center of the
scrollable container, positioning it in the middle of the visible area.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]end\f[R]: Aligns the element\(cqs bottom edge with the bottom of
the scrollable container, placing the element at the end of the visible
area vertically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nearest\f[R]: Scrolls the element to the nearest edge in the
vertical direction.
If the element is closer to the top edge of the scrollable container, it
will align to the top; if it\(cqs closer to the bottom edge, it will
align to the bottom.
This minimizes the scrolling distance.
The default is \f[CR]start\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]container\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Defines the scrollable ancestor container.
Its value can be one of the following:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]all\f[R]: All scrollable containers are impacted (including the
viewport).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nearest\f[R]: Only the nearest scrollable container is impacted by
the scroll.
The default is \f[CR]all\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]inline\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Defines the horizontal alignment of the element within the scrollable
ancestor container.
Its value can be one of the following:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]start\f[R]: Aligns the element\(cqs left edge with the left of the
scrollable container, making the element appear at the start of the
visible area horizontally.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]center\f[R]: Aligns the element horizontally at the center of the
scrollable container, positioning it in the middle of the visible area.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]end\f[R]: Aligns the element\(cqs right edge with the right of the
scrollable container, placing the element at the end of the visible area
horizontally.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nearest\f[R]: Scrolls the element to the nearest edge in the
horizontal direction.
If the element is closer to the left edge of the scrollable container,
it will align to the left; if it\(cqs closer to the right edge, it will
align to the right.
This minimizes the scrolling distance.
The default is \f[CR]nearest\f[R].
.RE
.RE
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that fulfills with an object containing the
following property:
.TP
\f[B]interrupted\f[R]
A boolean value indicating whether the scrolling operation was
interrupted (\f[CR]true\f[R]) or not (\f[CR]false\f[R]).
Such an interruption typically happens when a programmatic scroll is
ongoing, and another programmatic scroll is initiated on the same
element before the first one finishes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] element = document.getElementById(\(dqbox\(dq);

element.scrollIntoView();
element.scrollIntoView(\f[B]false\f[R]);
element.scrollIntoView({ block: \(dqend\(dq });
element.scrollIntoView({ behavior: \(dqsmooth\(dq, block: \(dqend\(dq, inline: \(dqnearest\(dq });
.EE
.SS Controlling top/bottom alignment
By default, the element is aligned to the top (or bottom) edge of the
scrollable ancestor.
To define a custom spacing, use \f[CR]scroll\-margin\-top\f[R] or
\f[CR]scroll\-margin\-bottom\f[R].
This is often useful when there\(cqs a fixed header on the page.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<body>
  <header class=\(dqnavbar\(dq>Navbar</header>
  <main class=\(dqcontent\(dq>
    <button id=\(dqgo\-to\-bottom\(dq>Go to bottom</button>
    <button id=\(dqgo\-to\-top\(dq>Go to top</button>
  </main>
</body>
.EE
.SS CSS
.IP
.EX
\&.navbar {
  height: 50px;
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  border\-bottom: 1.5px solid black;
  display: flex;
  justify\-content: center;
  align\-items: center;
}
\&.content {
  height: 2000px;
  position: relative;
}
go\-to\-bottom {
  position: absolute;
  top: 10px;
  /* Without this, the button will be aligned to the top of the page
  instead of bottom of navbar when scrolled */
  scroll\-margin\-top: 60px;
}
go\-to\-top {
  position: absolute;
  bottom: 10px;
  scroll\-margin\-bottom: 0;
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
.IP
.EX
const goToTop = document.getElementById(\(dqgo\-to\-top\(dq);
const goToBottom = document.getElementById(\(dqgo\-to\-bottom\(dq);
goToBottom.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () => {
  goToTop.scrollIntoView({ behavior: \(dqinstant\(dq, block: \(dqend\(dq });
});
goToTop.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () => {
  goToBottom.scrollIntoView({ behavior: \(dqinstant\(dq, block: \(dqstart\(dq });
});
.EE
.SS Result
.SS Responding to the end of the scroll
Our \c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/scroll-promises/element-methods/
element methods demo
.UE \c
\ (\c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/tree/main/scroll-promises/element-methods
see source code
.UE \c
) demonstrates how the promise return value of
\f[CR]scrollIntoView()\f[R] can be used to respond to the end of a
scrolling operation.
This technique is mostly useful in cases where the scrolling occurs
smoothly over time (achieved by setting the \f[CR]behavior\f[R] option
to \f[CR]smooth\f[R], or by setting the scrolling element\(cqs
\f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] property to \f[CR]smooth\f[R]).
.SS HTML
Our HTML includes a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element containing several
paragraphs of content and a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element toolbar containing
\f[CR]<button>\f[R] elements that trigger various scrolling operations
on the \f[CR]<section>\f[R].
The last paragraph has an \f[CR]id\f[R] of \f[CR]end\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\(dq>scroll() to 1000</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-to\(dq>scrollTo() top</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-by\(dq>scrollBy() 200</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-into\-view\(dq>Scroll last &lt;p&gt; into view</\f[B]button\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>

<\f[B]section\f[R]>
  ...

  <\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqend\(dq>...</\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
We give the \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element a fixed \f[CR]height\f[R] and
an \f[CR]overflow\-y\f[R] value of \f[CR]scroll\f[R] so that it scrolls
vertically, and set its \f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] property to
\f[CR]smooth\f[R] so that any scroll operations are animated smoothly
over time rather than instantly.
.IP
.EX
section {
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 1px solid black;
  \f[B]padding\f[R]: 20px;
  \f[B]margin\-top\f[R]: 60px;
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 500px;
  \f[B]overflow\-y\f[R]: scroll;
  \f[B]scroll\-behavior\f[R]: smooth;
}
.EE
.PP
We also create two class selectors; when a \f[CR]fade\-out\f[R] or
\f[CR]fade\-in\f[R] class is applied to an element, an
\f[CR]animation\f[R] is applied so that it smoothly fades out or in,
respectively.
We also define \f[CR]\(atkeyframes\f[R] blocks to define the required
\f[CR]opacity\f[R] changes for those animations.
.IP
.EX
\&.fade\-out {
  \f[B]animation\f[R]: fade\-out 0.3s linear both;
}

\&.fade\-in {
  \f[B]animation\f[R]: fade\-in 0.3s linear both;
}

\f[B]\(atkeyframes\f[R] fade\-out {
  from {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 1;
  }

  to {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 0;
  }
}

\f[B]\(atkeyframes\f[R] fade\-in {
  from {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 0;
  }

  to {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 1;
  }
}
.EE
.PP
The rest of the CSS is not shown, for brevity.
.SS JavaScript
We start by grabbing references to the \f[CR]<button>\f[R] that runs the
\f[CR]scrollIntoView()\f[R] operation, the toolbar \f[CR]<div>\f[R], and
the paragraph with an \f[CR]id\f[R] of \f[CR]end\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] scrollIntoViewBtn = document.querySelector(\(dq.scroll\-into\-view\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] toolbar = document.querySelector(\(dqdiv\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] end = document.querySelector(\(dq#end\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Next, we define a function called \f[CR]isInterrupted()\f[R], designed
to run in response to a scroll operation finishing, which takes a
boolean \f[CR]interrupted\f[R] value as a parameter.
It logs a message to the console to say that scrolling is finished and
indicate whether the operation was interrupted (\f[CR]interrupted\f[R]
is \f[CR]true\f[R]) or not.
In addition, if \f[CR]interrupted\f[R] is \f[CR]true\f[R], it calls an
\f[CR]alert()\f[R] to clearly indicate the interruption.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] isInterrupted(interrupted) {
  console.log(\(gaScroll finished;${interrupted ? \(dq \(dq : \(dq not \(dq}interrupted\(ga);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (interrupted) {
    alert(\(dqScroll interrupted!\(dq);
  }
}
.EE
.PP
When the button is clicked, we immediately apply the
\f[CR]fade\-out\f[R] class to the toolbar, causing it to fade out.
We then run \f[CR]scrollIntoView()\f[R] on the end paragraph to cause
the \f[CR]<section>\f[R] to scroll until the end paragraph is in view,
awaiting its promise resolution as we do so and storing the
\f[CR]result\f[R] in a constant.
When the promise has resolved, we call \f[CR]isInterrupted()\f[R] to
report that the scroll operation has finished and whether it was
interrupted.
Finally, we apply the \f[CR]fade\-in\f[R] class to the toolbar, causing
it to fade back in again.
.IP
.EX
scrollIntoViewBtn.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  toolbar.className = \(dqfade\-out\(dq;
  \f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] end.scrollIntoView();
  isInterrupted(result.interrupted);
  toolbar.className = \(dqfade\-in\(dq;
});
.EE
.PP
The code not relevant to \f[CR]scrollIntoView()\f[R] is not shown, for
brevity.
.SS Result
Click the buttons to see the scrolling behavior.
Note how the toolbar fades out when a button is pressed, and fades in
again once the smooth scrolling is finished.
Also try pressing one button and then quickly pressing another button
before the first scrolling operation has finished.
Note how, in these cases, the scrolling is reported as interrupted.
.PP
You can also \c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/scroll-promises/element-methods/
load the demo in a separate tab
.UE \c
\ and view the \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/tree/main/scroll-promises/element-methods
source code
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Aside on feature detection
If you run this example in a browser that doesn\(cqt support
promise\-returning scroll operations, the scroll operations are still
smooth, but the toolbar doesn\(cqt fade out and then fade back in once
the operation is finished.
The feature detection is handled by a function called
\f[CR]supportsScrollPromises()\f[R], which runs a scroll operation and
tests whether its return value is a promise:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] supportsScrollPromises() {
  \f[B]const\f[R] test = section.scroll(0, 0);
  \f[B]return\f[R] test \f[B]instanceof\f[R] Promise;
}
.EE
.PP
Check out the \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/blob/main/scroll-promises/element-methods/index.js
source code
.UE \c
\ to see how the feature detection is used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.scrollIntoViewIfNeeded()\f[R] \f[I](non\-standard)\f[R]
